      "id": 39,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Libris",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "The oldest following in the series: in the year 12, after the counted age's first building collapses, the stone trades formed a body that could answer for the ground. Its priests all hold trades, its central rite is the Grounding verdict no king can overrule, and its unanswered prayers are kept in bound books until the answer arrives, generations later if necessary.",
      "body": "The faith of [[Libris]] is the oldest organized following in this series, older than any patron's church, and it shows: it has the fewest priests who are only priests, the fewest doctrines, and the longest memory of what it is for. The ground was being worshipped before the peoples were finished being shaped, because the ground was already there, under every foot, holding.\n\n## Before the church\n\nStone-workers were keeping Libris's customs before anyone called them a faith. Quarrymen left the first cut of a new face unworked overnight, in the belief that the stone should be asked before it was taken. Masons blessed foundation stones and cursed, professionally and in writing, any builder who skimped one. Gravediggers set their stones facing the deep. Miners in the furthest dark kept the quiet, speaking low past the point where the lamps give out, where the rock sometimes sounds as if it is listening. The dwarves' forge-priests served Libris alongside [[Patrons|Tharnok]] at every forge. None of it was organized, and none of it needed to be, until buildings started falling down in the counted age and killing people in job lots.\n\nThe early count brought a building boom: bridges, towers, granaries, whole towns of stone raised by peoples in a hurry. Stone does not hurry, and it does not forgive, and a wall badly founded may stand for the builder's whole life and fall on his grandchildren. In the first decade of the count it fell on the builders instead, twice in one year in two different kingdoms, and both times the enquiry produced the same empty circle: the quarry blamed the dressing, the dressing blamed the mortar, the mortar blamed the ground, and the ground, whose whole faith this was, had no one empowered to answer.\n\n## The settling\n\nIn the year 12 of the First Age, the stone trades of three kingdoms did what Tharnok's clerks were about to do for the Ledger and did it for the ground: they formed a body that could answer. Master masons, quarrymasters, gravediggers, and mine delvers agreed on a common charge and a common rite, and the Church of Libris dates its settling from that agreement, the earliest of any in this series.\n\nThe rite is the Grounding, and it has never changed. Before a foundation is dug, the ground-priests walk the site, dig their trial pits, taste the soil and strike the stone, and say whether the ground will bear what is planned, and if it will not, the work does not proceed under the church's blessing, whatever the patron paying for it says. Kings have raged at a Grounding verdict. The verdicts are remembered, in the church's keeping, alongside the rather longer list of kings who built anyway, with dates, and outcomes.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nFoundations, first: the Grounding of every serious building in the lands where the church is kept, and the certification of the masons who lay them. A foundation that holds is what a blessing from Libris looks like, simple and total, and whole towns stand on answered prayers of exactly that kind.\n\nBoundaries, second. The church sets and keeps boundary stones, between fields, kingdoms, and graves, and a boundary stone blessed by its priests may be old, mossed, and half-buried, but moving one is treated by every court on the continent as moving the land itself. The church's memory for old boundaries is one of the reasons treaties cite its priests as witnesses: stone outlasts the clerks who argue about it.\n\nGraves, third. The church digs for every faith and sets the stones for most of them, and keeps the old rule that a grave faces the deep. It does not ferry the dead, no Element faith does: the roads belong to the patrons' churches. It prepares the body's last foundation, and considers that charge enough. See [[The Settling of the Shared Church]].\n\nAnd the deeps, last: the customs of the mines are the church's liturgy in working clothes. The quiet underground, the listening watch at the face, the leaving of the deep places as they were found. To dig down is to dig toward the heart of the world, where the god sleeps cradled in the shard's deepest layers, and the church's whole doctrine about ground-quakes is carried in a tavern saying its priests will neither confirm nor deny: tread lightly on the Cradle, lest you wake the child. When the ground moves, the church rings no bell and says no prayer aloud. It listens, with everybody else, to hear whether it settles.\n\n## Priests with trades\n\nThe church's oddity is that it barely has a clergy. No one may be ordained a ground-priest who does not hold a mastery in a stone trade, quarry, masonry, grave work, or the deep mines, and its priests work their trades all their lives, taking their turns at the Groundings and the boundary ridings as those duties come. Ordination adds a vigil to the mastery: one night alone at the deepest face the candidate has worked, in the dark, listening. There is no examination afterward. The other priests simply note, over the following years, whether the candidate came back up patient.\n\nFor Libris answers at the pace of stone, and the church has built its customs around that fact rather than apologizing for it. A prayer offered today may be answered in a grandchild's lifetime, and the church keeps the petitions it has not yet seen answered in bound books that pass from priest to priest like unfinished buildings. When an answer comes, late by every other faith's reckoning, the church sends word to the petitioner's descendants, by name, however many generations down the line the answer has traveled. Families keep these letters the way they keep foundation stones: as the proof, produced at need, that something in their house was built on ground that held.\n\n## The church today\n\nIt is everywhere and almost invisible, a faith whose temples are quarries and churchyards and the deep stations of mines, whose services are verdicts and ridings and the quiet. It feuds, slowly and on principle, with the faith of [[Water]], whose patience wears where Libris bears, and whose congregations it meets at every coast and riverbank with the resignation of a wall that knows the long odds. See [[The Settling of the Church of Water]].\n\nThe next faith in this series is the ground's other old neighbor, the one that melts its work in the deep and rises through its mountains. See [[The Settling of the Church of Fire]].",
