      "id": 40,
      "title": "DM Draft: The Settling of the Church of Fire",
      "type": "Organization",
      "categoryId": 4,
      "summary": "In the year 61 a forge-town's perpetual hearth died on a festival night because everyone assumed someone else was watching it. The relighting, from a forge fire and a hearth fire together, Theft and Gift in alternating watches with the founding question left permanently open, is the church's settling, and its absence-from-post law is the oldest fire code on the continent.",
      "body": "Every other faith in this series was settled by people who agreed about something. The Church of [[Fire]] was settled by people who agreed to stop needing to agree, and to keep a flame lit together in the meantime, and it has been keeping it lit, in shifts, for over a thousand years, just in case the question is ever asked.\n\n## Before the church\n\nFire is the one Element mortals can make, and from the beginning its followers split over how they came to be able to. One tradition says fire-making was stolen, and every hearth is lit with stolen goods. The other says it was taught, the one lesson the shard ever gave directly, and every hearth is a thank-you. Smiths above all kept the argument, and cooks, bakers, glassblowers, charcoal burners, and the tenders of funeral pyres kept their own versions of it, because fire-trades are where the question is not abstract. Both camps kept a fire burning at all times. Neither camp would eat at the other's.\n\nThere was no body to answer for any of it, and the lack showed in the usual way: not in doctrine but in disasters. Every town kept its own fire customs, its own watch, its own rules for kilns and thatch and the burying of ash, and a town that kept them badly burned with a regularity nobody accepted as the shard's will, exactly, but nobody was charged to prevent, either.\n\n## The settling\n\nIn the year 61 of the First Age, the perpetual hearth of a great forge-town went out. It was an old fire, kept alight by local pride since before the count, fed through wars and winters by an unbroken rotation of tenders, and it died during a festival night when the rotation failed, in the oldest way there is: everyone assumed someone else was watching it. Feed it, watch it, or bury it. There is no fourth way to keep a fire, and that night the town had managed a fourth way, which was to hold a parade.\n\nThe argument afterward nearly did what the fire had not. If fire was taught, the Gift camp said, the lesson had plainly been withdrawn from a town that stopped attending. If it was stolen, the Theft camp answered, the owner had simply repossessed it. Both camps were preparing to secede from each other's tables permanently when the town's smiths, who had a harvest of orders and no lit forge, proposed the arrangement the church still runs on. The hearth would be relit from two sources at once, a forge fire and a hearth fire, Theft and Gift together, and kept thereafter by both camps in alternating watches, under one roof, with the question of its origin formally and permanently left open, and the keeping of flames in the town made a charge somebody answered for.\n\nThe vote was not close. The Church of Fire dates its settling from that relighting, and its founding doctrine fits in the space the two camps could share: the fire must not go out, and nobody has to agree why it burns.\n\n## What they took charge of\n\nThe perpetual hearths, first: one public fire in every town where the church is kept, maintained in shifts around the clock, from which any household may take coals to relight its own. Before cheap sparks and in hard winters still, the town hearth is the difference between a cold hearth and a cold house, and the church's tenders are the only clergy on Libris whose absence from post is a criminal offense in most jurisdictions, a law written after the year 61 in the plainest possible ink.\n\nFire safety, second, which is the faith's true liturgy at civic scale: roof rules and kiln distances, chimney law, the firebreaks around granaries, the night watch, and the burying of ash, which is the third way in the smith's saying and the one householders forget. Fire-wardens of the church inspect, warn, and in most towns can order a roof stripped or a kiln closed. When a building burns anyway, the wardens' enquiry sorts it in the church's ancient categories: unfed, unwatched, or unburied, which between them cover every fire that has ever hurt anyone.\n\nAnd the pyres, third: fire's faithful tend the burning of the dead for every church that burns its dead, preparing the body's last unmaking, wood to heat and light and ash, the dead to smoke and memory. The church ferries no one, as no Element faith does. It performs the transformation and leaves the road to the patrons, and considers the division of labor obvious.\n\n## The count of answers\n\nThe church's oddity is that its central argument is its constitution, and it keeps the argument the way Tharnok's church keeps accounts. At ordination, every candidate is asked the founding question, stolen or taught, and must answer it sincerely, and the answer is recorded in the church's great tally, which has been kept since the settling and is now the longest continuous record of an opinion anybody owns. Candidates answer both ways. Both are ordained, in roughly equal numbers, century after century, and the tally is read aloud once a year at the great hearth, to general satisfaction, because the near-even count is the church's proof of its own doctrine: the question is alive, the fire is lit, and neither depends on the other being settled.\n\nIts priests, in consequence, argue constantly and part never. A Fire priest who stops arguing the question has not found peace. He has, in the church's judgment, let something go out.\n\n## The church today\n\nIts temples are working hearth-halls, forge, kitchen, and warmth under one soot-blackened roof, open at all hours because the fire is. Its wardens walk every town that keeps it. Its great enemies are not other faiths but its own element at full size, and against the faith of [[Water]] its contention is total and cheerfully returned, each unmaking the other on contact, drowned flame and vanished steam, with no truce and no shared ground anywhere in creation. See [[The Settling of the Church of Water]].\n\nIt settled in the year 61 around a dead hearth and a shared shift rota, the least mystical founding in this series and perhaps the most honest: a fire, kept, by people who disagree about everything except keeping it.",
